    {
        "slug": "frederick-banting",
        "title": "Frederick Banting's Toronto: Where Insulin Was Actually Discovered",
        "kicker": "A borrowed lab, a student assistant, and a summer of setbacks in 1921",
        "h1": "Frederick Banting",
        "dek": "Banting's famous late-night idea came in London, Ontario. The actual discovery — the lab work, the dogs, the summer of failed experiments that finally worked — happened at the University of Toronto.",
        "meta_desc": "Frederick Banting's Toronto career: his 1921 insulin research at the University of Toronto's Medical Building, his Toronto General Hospital clinic, and his burial at Mount Pleasant Cemetery.",
        "keywords": "Frederick Banting Toronto history, insulin discovery University of Toronto, Banting Institute",
        "hero_img": "F._G._Banting_1923.jpg",
        "hero_alt": "Dr. Frederick Banting, 1923",
        "hero_credit": "Frederick Banting, 1923 — Wikimedia Commons, public domain",
        "sections": [
            (None, '<p>Frederick Grant Banting was born in 1891 on a farm near Alliston, Ontario, well outside Toronto — his Toronto connection begins with his education, at Victoria College, University of Toronto, where he switched from divinity to medicine in 1912. After service in the First World War, Banting had his famous late-night idea about pancreatic extracts in 1920 while running a small practice in London, Ontario — not in Toronto. The actual discovery happened somewhere else entirely.</p>'),
            ("The summer of 1921", '<p>In spring 1921, Banting brought his idea to Professor J.J.R. Macleod, head of physiology at the University of Toronto, who gave him unused lab space in the university\'s Medical Building, a stock of experimental dogs, and a student assistant, Charles Best. Their first surgery on a dog was performed May 17, 1921. Through a difficult summer of setbacks, Banting and Best — later joined by biochemist James Collip — succeeded by autumn in producing a pancreatic extract that lowered blood sugar in diabetic dogs, announcing the discovery of <a href="medical-history.html">insulin</a> in 1922.</p>'),
            ("Toronto General Hospital and burial", '<p>In 1922 Banting joined the diabetic clinic at Toronto General Hospital, where the extract was first tested on human patients, while <a href="connaught-labs.html">Connaught Labs</a> scaled up production. In 1923 Banting and Macleod received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine; Banting controversially split his share of the prize money with Best. He was killed in a plane crash in Newfoundland in 1941, and was buried with military honours at <a href="cemeteries.html">Mount Pleasant Cemetery</a> in Toronto.</p>'),
        ],
        "sources": [
            ("The Canadian Encyclopedia — The Discovery of Insulin", "https://thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/the-discovery-of-insulin"),
            ("Dictionary of Canadian Biography — Banting, Sir Frederick Grant", "https://www.biographi.ca/en/bio/banting_frederick_grant_17E.html"),
        ],
        "related_words": [],
    },
    {
        "slug": "john-candy",
        "title": "John Candy: From East York to SCTV",
        "kicker": "A knee injury ended his football career; Second City started his real one",
        "h1": "John Candy",
        "dek": "Born in Newmarket and raised in East York, Candy trained at Toronto's Second City alongside Catherine O'Hara and Eugene Levy before SCTV made his characters famous across North America.",
        "meta_desc": "John Candy's Toronto roots: his East York childhood, his training at Second City Toronto, and the city's tributes including a named parkette and John Candy Day.",
        "keywords": "John Candy Toronto history, John Candy East York, Second City Toronto SCTV",
        "hero_img": "Toronto_skyline_(2012).jpg",
        "hero_alt": "The Toronto skyline",
        "hero_credit": "Toronto skyline — Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0",
        "sections": [
            (None, '<p>John Franklin Candy was born October 31, 1950 in Newmarket, Ontario, and raised in East York, then a separate borough now part of Toronto. He attended Neil McNeil Catholic High School, where a knee injury ended his football ambitions, before studying journalism at Centennial College.</p>'),
            ("Second City and SCTV", '<p>Candy joined Toronto\'s Second City improv troupe in the mid-1970s, training alongside Catherine O\'Hara, Eugene Levy, Dave Thomas and Rick Moranis. In October 1976, that ensemble launched SCTV, the sketch show that made Candy\'s characters — Johnny LaRue, Yosh Shmenge, Mayor Tommy Shanks — nationally known before the series was picked up by NBC in 1981.</p>'),
            ("Toronto\'s tributes", '<p>The city has marked Candy\'s local ties directly: a parkette at 769 Broadview Avenue in East York was officially named the John F. Candy Parkette in 2010, and Toronto has proclaimed October 31 as John Candy Day. Second City Toronto\'s smaller performance space is informally known as the "John Candy Box." He died in 1994, at age 43.</p>'),
        ],
        "sources": [
            ("Beach Metro Community News — Toronto Proclaims Oct. 31 as John Candy Day", "https://beachmetro.com/2020/10/31/toronto-proclaims-oct-31-as-john-candy-day/"),
            ("The Second City — John Candy", "https://www.secondcity.com/people/john-candy"),
        ],
        "related_words": [],
    },
